# 4. Treatment Planning (28).
CAT = "ncmhcePlanning"; SEG = "PLN"
Q = [
("A client with panic disorder wants \"to feel better.\" Which objective is written best?",
 "Ride a bus twice weekly by May", ["Feel calmer in daily life soon", "Understand her panic fully", "Have much less fear of buses"],
 "A good objective is specific, measurable and time bound. Riding the bus twice a week by a set date can be counted; feeling calmer cannot."),
("A client lists job loss, insomnia, marital conflict and heavy drinking. The counselor should first help him:",
 "Rank the problems with him", ["Work on all four at once", "Pick the easiest one alone", "Wait until he finds a job"],
 "The outline lists helping clients prioritize presenting problems and collaborating on goals. Safety concerns such as heavy drinking usually rise to the top."),
("A goal reads \"reduce depressive symptoms,\" and an objective reads \"PHQ-9 under 10 within 8 weeks.\" The objective is:",
 "A measurable step", ["A restatement of the goal", "A long-term outcome", "A diagnosis for billing"],
 "Goals state the broad outcome; objectives are measurable short-term steps that show progress toward it."),
("A client has no car and works two jobs. In the plan, these facts are best listed as:",
 "Practical barriers", ["Diagnostic criteria", "Client strengths", "Long-term goals"],
 "The outline asks counselors to identify barriers affecting goal attainment. Transport and work hours are practical barriers to plan around, for example with telehealth."),
("A client with social anxiety has a loyal sister and enjoys painting. The counselor uses these as:",
 "Strengths to build on", ["Barriers to progress", "Symptoms to monitor", "Signs of his avoidance"],
 "Identifying strengths that improve the chance of reaching goals is part of treatment planning. A supportive sister and a valued hobby can be built into steps."),
("A client in outpatient care keeps relapsing to daily cocaine use despite weekly sessions. The plan should consider:",
 "A more intensive level of care", ["Ending services as noncompliant", "Fewer sessions each month for now", "Waiting for a crisis"],
 "When outpatient care is not enough, referral to intensive outpatient or another higher level of care is indicated. Ending services abandons the client."),
("A client with depression also has untreated hypothyroidism. The plan should include:",
 "Medical referral alongside it", ["Ignoring the medical issue", "Diagnosing the thyroid", "Waiting until therapy ends"],
 "The outline lists referring to others for concurrent treatment. Thyroid problems can cause depressive symptoms, so medical care runs alongside counseling."),
("A client says her drinking is fine and she sees no reason to talk about it. In the stages of change she is in:",
 "Precontemplation", ["Contemplation", "Preparation", "Relapse"],
 "Precontemplation is no intention to change in the foreseeable future, often without seeing a problem. Contemplation brings awareness with ambivalence."),
("A counselor will be on leave for six weeks. For a client at ongoing risk, she should arrange:",
 "Coverage by a colleague", ["A pause in all contact", "Weekly email check-ins", "A new diagnosis first"],
 "Planning for a disruption of service, such as coverage by a named colleague with the client's consent, keeps care continuous for clients who need it."),
("A client has met her goals and feels steady. The counselor should begin to:",
 "Begin planning the ending", ["End without any discussion", "Add goals to keep going", "Refer her elsewhere"],
 "Termination is planned with the client when goals are met, reviewing gains and how she will keep them. Adding goals only to continue is not indicated."),
("Which sign best shows a client is ready for discharge?",
 "Goals met and held", ["A full insurance year", "Fewer than ten sessions", "The counselor's caseload"],
 "Readiness for discharge rests on the client meeting and maintaining treatment goals, not on calendar or administrative factors."),
("A client in recovery lists people, places and moods that lead to drinking, with a response for each. This is a:",
 "Relapse prevention", ["A behavior contract", "A crisis safety plan", "A discharge summary"],
 "Relapse prevention identifies high-risk situations and coping responses for each. A safety plan targets suicidal crises."),
("At session eight, a client's anxiety scores have not changed. The counselor should:",
 "Revisit goals and methods", ["Continue the same plan", "Discharge the client now", "Blame low motivation"],
 "The outline lists reviewing and revising the treatment plan. No progress after a fair trial is a reason to reassess goals and methods with the client."),
("A client's scores on the BDI-II drop from 30 to 12 over ten weeks. This is best used to:",
 "Show progress", ["Change the diagnosis", "Bill for more sessions", "End therapy that same day"],
 "Pre- and post-measures show whether treatment is working and can be shared with the client to review progress, as the outline lists."),
("A counselor writes, \"Client will attend AA because I think he needs it.\" The main flaw is:",
 "No collaboration", ["It is too measurable", "It is too specific", "It names a resource"],
 "The outline stresses collaborating with clients on goals. A plan imposed by the counselor tends to lower engagement and follow-through."),
("A client from a culture that values family decisions wants her parents involved in planning. The counselor should:",
 "Include them, with consent", ["Insist she decide alone", "Refuse any contact with family", "Let her parents set the goals"],
 "Integrating the client's culture into the plan respects her values. Her consent keeps her at the center; parents setting the goals would sideline her."),
("A client also sees a physician and a physical therapist. Before calling them, the counselor needs:",
 "A signed release for each", ["A court order for records", "The client's verbal hint", "The physician's permission"],
 "Collaborating with other providers requires the client's written authorization for each one. A court order is not needed, and the physician cannot consent for the client."),
("A teen's plan says \"stop fighting at school.\" A better behavioral objective is:",
 "Zero fights in four weeks", ["Become a nicer person", "Respect all teachers more", "Feel less angry inside"],
 "Behavioral objectives name an observable behavior and a time frame, so the teen, family and school can track it."),
("A counselor reviews the plan with a client every 30 days. This mainly allows them to:",
 "Track progress", ["Lengthen treatment", "Satisfy curiosity", "Revise the diagnosis"],
 "Planned reviews engage the client in looking at progress toward goals and adjusting the plan, as the outline lists."),
("A client keeps missing sessions because of shifting work hours. A fitting plan change is:",
 "Flexible or video sessions", ["Closing the case at once instead", "A late fee every time", "Weekly reminder letters only"],
 "Addressing barriers to attendance keeps the client engaged. Flexible scheduling or telehealth removes the barrier rather than penalizing it."),
("A client was just discharged from a hospital after a suicide attempt. The counselor should arrange:",
 "Contact within days", ["A visit next month", "A call only if he asks", "No contact, for his privacy"],
 "The period right after discharge carries high suicide risk. Prompt follow-up after discharge is a listed treatment planning task."),
("A client with insomnia agrees to log bedtimes, wakings and naps each day. This log is a:",
 "Sleep diary", ["Behavior contract", "Thought record", "Mood chart"],
 "A sleep diary gives a baseline and tracks change, and it guides sleep interventions. A thought record logs automatic thoughts instead."),
("Testing shows a client's strongest interests are in helping and teaching. The counselor uses this to:",
 "Inform her choices", ["Diagnose a disorder", "Set her session fee", "Replace counseling"],
 "The outline lists using assessment results to facilitate client decision-making. Interest results inform choices; they do not diagnose."),
("A client asks why she must practice skills between sessions. The counselor should:",
 "Explain why practice helps", ["Drop homework from the plan", "Tell her it is required", "Ignore the question"],
 "Educating the client about the value of following the plan increases engagement. Explaining the reason works better than insisting."),
("A client is ready to end weekly counseling but fears losing her gains. A common option is:",
 "Booster sessions later", ["Weekly sessions for years", "Starting a new problem list", "Ending with no contact"],
 "Scheduled booster sessions after termination help clients maintain progress while still ending regular treatment."),
("A group member will leave in three weeks when she moves. The leader should:",
 "Discuss the exit as a group", ["Let her leave unannounced", "Stop the group early", "Replace her the same day"],
 "Discussing transitions in group membership lets members process the change. Leaving unannounced can unsettle the group."),
("A client with a terminal illness wants to focus on mending ties with his son. The plan should:",
 "Center his own goal", ["Focus on his diagnosis only", "Avoid end-of-life topics", "Set a five-year goal for him"],
 "Collaborative planning starts from the client's own priorities. End-of-life work often centers on relationships and meaning."),
("A client lives two hours from any clinic and has no internet. Before choosing a modality, the counselor should:",
 "Weigh access to each option", ["Require weekly office visits", "Use video sessions anyway", "Decline to treat him"],
 "The outline lists determining the appropriate modality of treatment. Practical access, such as phone sessions, shapes which modality will work."),
]
